In this lesson we'll look at what it really means to solve equations simultaneously. When you solve two equations together, you're looking for values that make both of them true at the same time. Picture each linear equation as a straight line on a graph, and a solution is just a point that sits on every line you're working with — basically where they cross. Now, two lines can only relate in three ways. If they have different slopes, they'll cross exactly once, giving you one solution. If they're parallel with the same slope but different starting heights, they never meet, so there's no solution at all. And if they have the same slope and the same starting height, they're actually the same line, which means every point works and you get infinitely many solutions. The same idea stretches to curves too. When you match a curve against a straight line, the solutions are just the spots where they intersect. A line can slice through a parabola in two points, touch it at exactly one point like a tangent, or miss it completely. Now let's see it in action.